## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design end-to-end tests for offline-first collaborative applications—a common pattern in modern productivity tools. It evaluates understanding of multi-client synchronization, conflict resolution strategies, and proper test isolation.

## Key Concepts
- **Offline-first syncing and eventual consistency**: Understanding how data propagates when clients reconnect
- **Conflict resolution strategies**: CRDT (Conflict-free Replicated Data Types) ensures deterministic merging; last-writer-wins uses timestamps
- **Multi-client test isolation**: Using separate browser contexts to simulate concurrent users
- **Network simulation**: Leveraging tools like Playwright's network throttling to emulate offline/online transitions
- **Per-note permissions and presence**: Validating access control and real-time user indicators

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright setup with two isolated contexts
const { chromium } = require('playwright');

const browser = await chromium.launch();
const context1 = await browser.newContext();
const context2 = await browser.newContext();

const user1 = await context1.newPage();
const user2 = await context2.newPage();

// Seed shared note
const noteId = await createSharedNote(user1, 'Test Note');
await user2.goto(`/notes/${noteId}`);

// Take user1 offline
await context1.setOffline(true);

// User1 edits offline
await user1.fill('.editor', 'User1 offline edit');

// User2 edits online
await user2.fill('.editor', 'User2 online edit');

// Restore connectivity
await context1.setOffline(false);

// Verify sync and conflict resolution
const finalContent = await user1.textContent('.editor');
console.log('Final content:', finalContent);
```

## Purpose

This question evaluates practical E2E testing skills, specifically around runtime feature flags, test data seeding, and UI assertions in a social application context.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flag rollout strategies
- Test data seeding for user cohorts
- Mocking remote config services
- UI assertions for different feature states
- Stability under mid-flight config changes

## Code Example

```javascript
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('feature-flag gated composer', async ({ page, context }) => {
  // Seed users and mock remote config
  const userA = await seedUser({ cohort: 0 });
  const userB = await seedUser({ cohort: 1 });

  await context.addCookies([
    { name: 'user_id', value: userA.id, domain: 'localhost' },
    { name: 'cohort', value: '0', domain: 'localhost' }
  ]);

  // Mock remote config for 10% rollout to cohort 1
  await page.route('**/remote-config', async (route) => {
    route.fulfill({ json: { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 10 } } });
  });

  // Verify cohort 0 sees old composer
  await page.goto('/home');
  await expect(page.locator('.old-composer')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.new-composer')).not.toBeVisible();

  // Post and verify
  await page.fill('.tweet-input', 'Tweet from cohort 0');
  await page.click('.submit-tweet');
  await expect(page.locator('.tweet:has-text("Tweet from cohort 0")')).toBeVisible();
});

test('mid-session config flip', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.addInitScript(() => {
    window.__INITIAL_CONFIG__ = { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 0 } };
  });

  await page.goto('/home');
  await expect(page.locator('.old-composer')).toBeVisible();

  // Flip config mid-session
  await page.addInitScript(() => {
    window.__REMOTE_CONFIG__ = { composer_rollout: { cohort: 1, percentage: 100 } };
  });
  await page.reload();

  await expect(page.locator('.new-composer')).toBeVisible();
});
```

